<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Khanate of Urmia</h1><p class="st-meta">1747 CE – 1797 CE (approx.) · capital Urmia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A khanate of the Afshar tribe on the western shore of Lake Urmia, whose khan Fath Ali Khan Afshar contended for power in Iran after Nader Shah's death.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urmia">Urmia</a> was home to a branch of the Turkic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afshar-tribe">Afshar tribe</a>, kin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a>. After Nader's assassination in 1747 and the break-up of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire</a>, Afshar chiefs held Urmia and its plain as a khanate.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fath-ali-khan-afshar">Fath Ali Khan Afshar</a>, the most ambitious of the khans, fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azad-khan-afghan">Azad Khan Afghan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan Zand</a> for control of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Karim Khan besieged and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urmia">Urmia</a> in 1763, and Fath Ali Khan was later killed. The khanate continued under other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afshar-tribe">Afshar</a> khans as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zand-dynasty">Zand</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>. Its population included Azerbaijani Turks, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a> and Assyrian Christians, and the plain produced grain, fruit and tobacco.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agha-mohammad-khan-qajar">Agha Mohammad Khan</a>'s <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urmia">Urmia</a> in the 1790s, and by 1797 the khanate was incorporated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> province of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="khanate, 1747 CE – 1797 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M18 74 V58 Q50 30 82 58 V74 Z"/><path d="M42 74 V60 H58 V74"/><path d="M30 50 L50 36 L70 50"/><path d="M50 36 V26"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">khanate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesopotamia and Iran</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:94.47%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1747 CE – 1797 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Urmia</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Fath Ali Khan Afshar</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shia Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Azerbaijani Turkic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Iran</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Urmia Khanate</dd></dl></aside></div>
